---
import LessonLayout   from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader   from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonCallout  from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonDiagram  from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
---

<LessonLayout title="2.3 — Vectors, Embeddings & Attention">

  <LessonHeader
    num="2.3"
    title="Vectors, Embeddings & Attention"
    subtitle="What a vector is, what an embedding is, and how a word's position shifts with context."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">A Vector Is Just a Position</h2>

    <p>
      One number places something on a line. Say the number is 5 — now you know exactly where
      that thing sits on a one-dimensional line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two numbers place something on a flat map: five steps right, three steps up. That pair of
      numbers is already a <strong>vector</strong> — a set of numbers that tells you where
      something is in a space.
    </p>

    <p>
      A third number puts that same point in 3D space. Keep adding numbers — hundreds, thousands
      — and you're locating something in a space too large to draw. The picture stops being
      possible to visualize, but the idea never changes: a vector is a position, nothing more.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">A Word Doesn't Get One Number — It Gets a Position</h2>

    <p>
      A word like <code>cat</code> doesn't get a single number. It gets a position — a vector —
      somewhere inside a learned space of meaning. Nearby positions mean related ideas:
      <code>cat</code>, <code>dog</code>, and <code>kitten</code> end up close together.
      <code>car</code> sits far from all three — different meaning, different neighborhood.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-map">
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:62%;top:24%;" data-label="cat"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:70%;top:32%;" data-label="kitten"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:56%;top:36%;" data-label="dog"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:18%;top:72%;" data-label="car"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:28%;top:80%;" data-label="truck"></span>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption"><code>cat</code>, <code>kitten</code>, and <code>dog</code> land close together. <code>car</code> and <code>truck</code> land in a different neighborhood entirely.</p>

    <p>
      Nobody draws this map by hand. During training, words used in similar contexts get pushed
      closer together; words used in different contexts drift apart — a side effect of the model
      getting better at predicting text, not something anyone designed directly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Chain: Token → ID → Vector</h2>

    <p>
      Three words, precisely defined, because they get confused for each other constantly:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Token</strong> — a piece of text.</li>
      <li><strong>Token ID</strong> — just an address. A row number in a table. It carries no
        meaning by itself, the same way a house number doesn't describe the house.</li>
      <li><strong>Embedding</strong> — the learned vector sitting at that row. <em>This</em> is
        where meaning actually lives.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>Token <code>"cat"</code> → id <code>4291</code> → row <code>4291</code> of the embedding
        matrix → <code>[0.12, -0.44, 0.91, …]</code>. That row <em>is</em> the embedding. The
        model never touches the word "cat" directly — only the number, and then the vector that
        number points to.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Training: Building a Geometry, Not Memorizing a Dictionary</h2>

    <p>
      Before training, every row of that table is a random vector — the map means nothing yet.
      Training repeatedly nudges those numbers to reduce prediction error, over and over, across
      enormous amounts of text. <strong>Weights</strong> are exactly this: the numbers inside the
      embedding table (and every matrix downstream of it), adjusted by training until they're
      useful.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The model isn't memorizing a dictionary">
      <p>
        It's building a geometry of the language. Nobody tells it <code>cat</code> and
        <code>dog</code> are related — that closeness emerges because both words keep showing up
        in similar contexts, and training keeps nudging their positions toward each other as a
        side effect of getting better at prediction.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <p>
      Why go to the trouble of turning words into positions at all? Because <strong>math cannot
      operate on meaning directly — it can operate on positions.</strong> You can't compute "how
      similar are these two sentences," but you can compute the distance between two vectors. You
      can't "70%-blend" two words, but you can take 70% of one vector and 30% of another and get
      a new, meaningful vector back. Turning words into numbers isn't a technicality — it's what
      makes every later step in this lesson possible at all.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">One Embedding, Many Contexts</h2>

    <p>
      Here's the limit of everything so far: the embedding table gives <code>bank</code> exactly
      <strong>one</strong> starting position, the same every time, regardless of sentence. But
      "I'm going to the <em>bank</em> to deposit money" and "I'm sitting on the <em>bank</em> of a
      river" clearly don't mean the same thing. Something has to move <code>bank</code>'s position
      differently depending on what's actually around it — and that something is
      <strong>attention</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-map">
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:48%;top:48%;" data-label="bank (start)"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:75%;top:20%;" data-label="river"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:82%;top:30%;" data-label="water"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:18%;top:75%;" data-label="deposit"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:26%;top:85%;" data-label="account"></span>
      <svg class="l0008-path-svg" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none">
        <polyline points="48,48 74,26" class="l0008-path-line" />
        <polyline points="48,48 24,78" class="l0008-path-line" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">Same starting position. "…bank of a river" pulls it toward river/water. "…bank to deposit money" pulls it toward deposit/account. Attention is what moves it, and which direction it moves depends entirely on the words actually present.</p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Attention — Query, Key, Value</h2>

    <p>
      To decide which direction to move, the model computes three things for every word:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Query</strong> — what <code>bank</code> is currently looking for, given where it sits.</li>
      <li><strong>Key</strong> — what each nearby word is offering.</li>
      <li><strong>Value</strong> — the actual content that word contributes if it's a good match.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Compare the query against every key — a dot product, one number per comparison, higher when
      the two vectors point in a similar direction. Turn those comparisons into percentages
      (softmax), then blend every value using those percentages as weights. That blend is added
      to <code>bank</code>'s position, moving it — a small step, one layer's worth — toward
      whichever neighbors actually matched.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  Q["❓ bank's query&colon;<br/>what fits here?"]:::query
  K1["🏷️ river's key"]:::key
  K2["🏷️ deposit's key"]:::key
  Q -->|"85% match"| K1
  Q -->|"15% match"| K2
  K1 --> V1["💬 river's value"]:::value
  K2 --> V2["💬 deposit's value"]:::value
  V1 -->|"× 0.85"| OUT["🧭 direction to move&colon;<br/>mostly toward water"]:::out
  V2 -->|"× 0.15"| OUT

  classDef query fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef key    fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef value  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out    fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p class="l0008-caption">In "…bank of a river," river's key matches bank's query far better than deposit's does — so the blend leans almost entirely toward river's value.</p>

    <div class="l0008-math-note">
      <span class="l0008-math-tag">∑ in math terms</span>
      <p>Query, key, and value are each the input vector multiplied by a different fixed matrix —
        three different reshapings of the same starting point, each built for a different job
        (matching, offering, contributing). That's why the query-vs-key comparison can be a cheap
        dot product: both got moved into a shared space where "closeness" means "relevance," even
        though that wouldn't have meant anything on the raw embeddings.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What a Matrix Actually Is</h2>

    <p>
      A matrix is a grid of numbers — rows across, columns down. "Multiply by a matrix" means:
      for each output number, take one row, multiply it against the input position-by-position,
      add it up. One row = one independent, complete recipe.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-matrix-grid">
      <div class="l0008-matrix-col-label">2 columns — one per input number</div>
      <div class="l0008-matrix-row">
        <table class="l0008-matrix-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>col 1</th><th>col 2</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>row 1</th><td>0.5</td><td>1.2</td></tr>
            <tr><th>row 2</th><td>-0.3</td><td>0.9</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="l0008-matrix-row-label">2 rows —<br/>2 outputs</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Say <code>bank</code>'s blended position (the output of Attention above) is
      <code>[0.8, -0.2]</code>. One row of a matrix like the one above produces one output:
      <code>0.5×0.8 + 1.2×(-0.2) = 0.16</code>. A different row produces a different, independent
      output from that same input. More rows, more independent outputs — computed from the exact
      same numbers, in parallel. <strong>Weights</strong> are just the numbers filling that grid;
      training changes them, never the shape of the operation itself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Feedforward — A Second, Private Pass</h2>

    <p>
      Attention only ever produces a <em>blend</em> of existing information — a weighted average.
      It can't create a genuinely new combination on its own. After attention, every position runs
      through one more transformation, independently: multiply by a matrix (many small checks
      computed at once), zero out anything negative (keep only the checks that actually fired),
      multiply by a second matrix (fold the survivors back into one vector). That extra step —
      expand, cut, compress — is what lets the network represent things a pure blend can't.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Is this &quot;thinking&quot;?">
      <p>
        If "thinking" means a deterministic transformation of numbers, shaped by training — yes,
        precisely that is happening. If it means deliberation, awareness, or the ability to
        hesitate — no; the calculation runs forward once and that's the entire event, with no
        "inside" where anything like that could occur. The word gets used because the
        <em>output</em> resembles what thinking produces, not because the mechanism resembles how
        a person thinks.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Layers — Repeating the Whole Cycle</h2>

    <p>
      Stack that whole cycle — attention, then feedforward — many times, each with its own,
      separately-trained weights. Each pass refines <code>bank</code>'s position a little further,
      using whatever the previous pass already figured out.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  L1["Layer 1&colon;<br/>near water or money"]:::step --> L2["Layer 2&colon;<br/>specifically near a river"]:::step --> L3["Layer 3&colon;<br/>the sloped ground<br/>beside flowing water"]:::done

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      Run the same sentence's twin — "…bank to deposit money" — through the same three layers,
      and the path bends the other way at layer 1 and never recovers: "near water or money" →
      "specifically near a transaction" → "a financial institution." Same architecture, same
      number of layers, same fixed operations at each one — the only thing that differs is which
      neighbors actually matched at that first attention step.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Weights fixed, context free to change">
      <p>
        The weights that do this reshaping — every matrix, every layer — were set once, during
        training, and don't change again until the model is retrained. What's free to change is
        <strong>context</strong>: whichever words actually surround <code>bank</code> right now.
        Same fixed skill, applied fresh to whatever's actually in front of it — which is also why
        a model can incorporate a brand-new fact without retraining at all, simply by putting that
        fact in the context (the idea behind RAG, retrieval-augmented generation) rather than
        baking it into the weights.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Beam — Not Committing Too Early</h2>

    <p>
      If the surrounding words are genuinely ambiguous — a sentence that could plausibly continue
      toward either meaning — instead of committing to one direction immediately, the model can
      keep several candidate continuations alive and let more information settle which one
      actually pans out. Lesson 2.5 walks through this live, on a worked example.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Go Further</h2>
    <p>
      For real, interactive versions of these ideas running against actual trained models — not
      this lesson's illustrative numbers — see the collection at
      <a href="https://dentro.de/ai/visualizations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dentro.de/ai/visualizations</a>,
      in particular a live GPT-2 walkthrough with real attention visualization and a step-by-step
      breakdown of real text generation.
    </p>
  </div>

</LessonLayout>

<style>
  .l0008-caption {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* ── Map of Meaning ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .l0008-map {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    background: var(--bg2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .l0008-dot {
    position: absolute;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    margin-left: -5px;
    margin-top: -5px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--muted);
  }
  .l0008-dot--person { background: var(--accent); width: 12px; height: 12px; margin-left: -6px; margin-top: -6px; }
  .l0008-dot::after {
    content: attr(data-label);
    position: absolute;
    top: 14px;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem;
  }

  /* ── Narrowing-down path overlay (on top of .l0008-map) ─────────────── */
  .l0008-path-svg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .l0008-path-line {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent);
    stroke-width: 0.6;
    stroke-dasharray: 2 1.5;
  }

  /* ── Matrix grid (rows/columns) ──────────────────────────────────── */
  .l0008-matrix-grid {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-col-label {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-table th,
  .l0008-matrix-table td {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-table thead th,
  .l0008-matrix-table tbody th {
    background: var(--bg2);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .l0008-matrix-row-label {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  /* ── Math translation notes ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .l0008-math-note {
    background: var(--bg2);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .l0008-math-note p { margin-top: 0.4rem; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .l0008-math-tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
